#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.14"
# dependencies = [
#   "click>=8.1.0",
# ]
# ///

"""Regenerate port/reports/order-containers.md and port/reports/order-comparators.md (R18).

``port/audit-order`` walks the in-scope rows of ``port/manifest.tsv`` (read at run time; no
file list is written into this script or into a document) and writes two static-analysis
reports:

- ``port/reports/order-containers.md`` -- every unordered container
  (``std::unordered_map/set/multimap/multiset``, ``boost::unordered_*``,
  ``ankerl::unordered_dense::map/set``, ``tbb::concurrent_unordered_map/set``) that is
  **iterated** rather than only looked up.
- ``port/reports/order-comparators.md`` -- every comparator (an ``operator<``/``<=``/``>``
  definition, a ``std::map``/``set``/``multimap``/``multiset``/``priority_queue``'s ``Compare``
  argument, or a comparator passed at an ordering-algorithm call site) that is **not a total
  order**.

Both reports are fully derived: every cell is computed from the vendored tree on every run, none
is hand-edited, and running this script twice leaves both files byte-identical. Part 2 of this
step (18-divergences-regeneration) adds ``port/reports/divergences.md``, ``port/regenerate`` and
``just audit-check``; this script writes only the two reports above.

This is a heuristic static analyser, not a C++ parser. It masks out comments and string/char
literals, then recovers enough structure with brace matching (which header opened a given ``{``:
a class/struct body, a function body, or neither) to answer two questions per site: for a
container, is every one of its uses a lookup (``find``/``count``/``contains``/``at``/``[]``/
``insert``/``emplace``/``erase``-by-key/``size``/``empty``/``clear``), or does it get iterated
(range-``for``, ``begin``/``end``/``cbegin``/``cend``, or handed to an algorithm or a reduction)?
For a comparator, does its body match one of the three non-total-order shapes (a disjunction of
``<`` over different fields, a top-level ``<=``/``>=``, or a floating-point tolerance/``EPSILON``
compare), or one of the recognised total-order shapes (``std::tie(...) < std::tie(...)``, a
single-field compare, a nested lexicographic if-chain, or a nested ternary chain)?

This script also regenerates ``port/reports/divergences.md`` (task 18-divergences-regeneration,
part 2 of this step), from exactly four sources and no other: the rows of the two reports above
whose own ``diverges`` cell is ``yes``, read from this run's own in-memory tables rather than
from the files just written; every ``// FIDELITY(port):`` marker under ``crates/``; every row of
``port/reports/oracle-parity.tsv`` whose two oracles do not reach L1; and every row of
``port/reports/determinism.tsv`` whose gate is ``none``. The last two files are written by later
tasks and are absent today -- an absent source contributes zero rows and is not an error, but a
source that is present with an unreadable header (a renamed column) is. ``--out DIR`` redirects
all three reports to ``DIR`` instead of the committed ``port/reports/`` tree (what ``just
audit-check`` uses to regenerate into a scratch directory and diff); ``--self-test`` runs the
fixtures under ``port/tests/audit-order/`` and asserts their expected result.

**Every one of those four questions is answered conservatively.** Where the script cannot decide
whether a container is only looked up, it reports the row as iterated. Where it cannot decide
whether a comparator is a total order, it reports the row as not one. In both cases the reason
cell says the classification is undecided. The port's-choice mapping (``container_port_choice``
and ``comparator_port_choice``) and the diverges rule (``diverges``) are likewise written as
explicit, named branches below, not as
literals folded into a row dict, because the point of writing them in code is that a reviewer can
point at the branch that produced a given cell.

This script never attempts to establish whether a given site's order actually reaches compared
output -- that is a dynamic question a static audit cannot answer -- so ``diverges`` defaults to
``yes`` with a reason noting the reaches-output question is undecided, for every row this script
emits. The only branch that can answer ``no`` is a port's choice that provably carries no order at
all (a looked-up-only container, which is why that branch is never reached here: such containers
are not iterated, so no row is emitted for them). See ``diverges`` below for the one other named
exception the task describes (an already-strict relation under a non-strict textual shape), which
this static script has no way to detect and therefore never emits.
"""

